; EXERCISE 2: TWO ISLANDERS
;
; You meet two islanders, A and B (theory/island.kurt). Each part is a puzzle: find out who is a
; knight and who is a knave, first on paper, then prove it. The `show` lines give the answer away
; -- cover them while you solve the puzzle. Replace each `todo` by a proof; don't change the
; `show` lines. Check with `kurt 2-two-islanders.kurt`.

load theory/island
const A, B

; (a) A says: "We are both knaves."
;     Hint: assume that A is a knight and derive `false`. Then you know ¬knight(A), and A's
;     statement is false.
show (A says knave(A) ∧ knave(B)) ⇒ knave(A) ∧ knight(B)
proof
    todo
qed

; (b) A says: "At least one of us is a knave."
show (A says knave(A) ∨ knave(B)) ⇒ knight(A) ∧ knave(B)
proof
    todo
qed

; (c) A says: "B is a knight." B says: "A and I are of different kinds."
;     (B's statement is `knight(A) ⇔ knave(B)`: A is a knight exactly when B is a knave.)
show (A says knight(B)) ∧ (B says (knight(A) ⇔ knave(B))) ⇒ knave(A) ∧ knave(B)
proof
    todo
qed
